    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How was your death experience?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "After a suicide attempt that clinically took my life, I came back. What happened in those hours is the story I tell on stage — not the details, but the restoration. I share it because the room needs to know: the woman still struggling is not alone in the dark. There is a way through. There is a way out. If you or someone you love is struggling, call or text 988 — the Suicide & Crisis Lifeline (US)."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How did your faith and experience with God happen?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Slowly, then all at once. I spent years asking God 'Why did You save me?' before I realized I was asking the wrong question. The right one was 'What now?' Today my faith is less about perfect theology and more about a daily practice of showing up — prayer, scripture, community, obedience. I share that practice because it is portable. It works in a hospital room. It works in a small group. It works in the middle of a regular Tuesday."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the A.L.I.G.N. framework?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "A.L.I.G.N. is the five-step path I built for the woman who is still stuck. A — Awareness, naming what is keeping you there. L — Love Yourself, dissolving shame and self-sabotage. I — Invite God In, surrendering the outcome. G — Gratitude, rewiring the middle of the journey. N — Nurture Positivity, protecting what God is building. The letters spell the path out of stuck and into purpose."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How does awareness actually work?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Awareness is the first letter because nothing else works without it. Most of us are not stuck because we lack tools — we are stuck because we have not named the thing. Awareness is the practice of looking at the pattern, the lie, the coping mechanism, the relationship, the voice — and refusing to flinch. It is not confession. It is not shame. It is the brave act of seeing clearly so God can begin the restoration."
        }
      }
    ]
  })}
